<p>This response is amazingly vehement for how wrong and baseless it is.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So…you're going to search for a single person named Mark in a population of 7 billion?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nope. Because the article also happens to let us know that the person in question lived in &quot;H█████, Indiana&quot;. So now our search is limited to towns that start with &quot;H&quot; in the US state of Indiana. That's not exactly &quot;the biggest fucking haystack ever&quot;, as you put it.</p>
<p>Even if we didn't have that, we also know that this person is from the same area as where &quot;Trudy D██████, a local real estate agent who had been reported missing on 07/22/2000&quot; is. Real estate agents don't exactly go missing all the time, and we also have a specific date and we know most of the person's name. Oh, and there's a town council member that we can also identify in this manner. Ten minute Google search.</p>
<p>If you're going to be all aggro and confrontational, with the &quot;don't you dare&quot;s and what have you, you had best come correct.</p>

